course: everyday
lesson: 217
title: Kitchen tools, the freezer, plugs and doors
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A kitchen is full of hidden dangers: a knife under soapy bubbles cannot be seen, a blender's blades can start again while it is plugged in, and loose sleeves or long hair can catch on a flame or a pan handle.
explain: Very cold things can hurt too: wet skin freezes onto frozen metal or an ice lolly straight from the freezer, and a can of drink left in the freezer can burst because water grows bigger when it freezes.
explain: Water and mains electricity must never meet; in the UK and Ireland bathrooms have pull-cord light switches for exactly this reason.
explain: Plugs are different around the world, so travellers use a proper travel adaptor instead of forcing a plug in.
explain: A girl in Sydney reaches into the washing-up for a spoon and finds a sharp knife instead; since then, her family washes knives separately and puts them away at once.
fact: Never leave a knife in soapy water or reach into water where a knife may be hidden.
fact: Before touching inside a blender, switch it off and unplug it.
fact: Never put hands or a spoon into a blender while it is running.
fact: To reach a high cupboard, use a steady step stool or ask someone tall, never a wheeled chair or the counter edge.
fact: Roll up sleeves and tie back long hair before cooking.
fact: You walk, never run, in the kitchen, because of knives, hot pans and slippery floors.
fact: Wet skin can freeze and stick to frozen metal or a very cold ice lolly.
fact: Water expands when it freezes, so a can of drink in the freezer can burst.
fact: Never use mains electrical things like a hair dryer near a full bath or sink.
fact: The UK and Ireland use plugs with three square pins, the USA and Canada two flat pins, and Australia and New Zealand slanted pins.
fact: Abroad, you use a proper travel adaptor rather than forcing a plug.
fact: Open swinging doors slowly and look through the glass if there is any, in case someone is behind.
exercise: After dinner you are washing up. Where should the sharp knife go? | Washed on its own and put away at once | At the bottom of the soapy water, out of the way | In the bubbles until the end | Under the plates to keep it safe
exercise: Why is a knife in soapy water dangerous? | Someone reaching in cannot see the blade | Soap makes knives sharper | The water makes the knife go rusty at once and break | The bubbles push the knife up
exercise: The blender is stuck. What do you do before poking inside? | Switch it off and unplug it | Poke it with a spoon while it runs | Switch it off but leave it plugged in | Shake it hard while it is on
exercise: Why must a blender be unplugged before you touch the blades? | It can start again if it is still plugged in | Unplugging makes the blades much less sharp to touch | Blenders only work when cold | The plug gets hot if left in
exercise: The biscuits are in a high cupboard. How do you reach them? | Use a steady step stool or ask someone tall | Stand on a chair with wheels | Climb onto the counter edge | Jump and grab the shelf
exercise: You are about to stir a pot on a gas cooker in a baggy jumper. What first? | Roll up your sleeves and tie back long hair | Stir quickly so your sleeve stays clear of the flame | Lean far over the pot to see it better as you stir | Turn the flame right up so that it cooks faster
exercise: Mum says "No running in the kitchen". Why? | Knives, hot pans and slippery floors make it dangerous | Running makes the food go cold | The kitchen floor is too small | Running scares the fridge's motor
exercise: You want to lick an ice lolly straight from the freezer. What is wise? | Let it warm a little first | Lick it at once while it is coldest | Hold it against your lips to warm it | Bite it hard so it warms up
exercise: Why can your tongue stick to frozen metal? | Wet skin freezes and sticks to it | Metal turns sticky when it gets very cold | Your tongue is magnetic | Frozen metal has glue on it
exercise: Your brother puts a can of lemonade in the freezer to chill it fast. What can happen if it is forgotten? | It can burst as the drink freezes and expands | It turns into lemonade powder | The bubbles disappear for ever | It shrinks and gets lighter
exercise: You want to dry your hair while your sister is in the bath. What is safe? | Dry it in another room away from water | Use the dryer only at the edge of the bath | Hold the dryer well up, high over the water | Use it quickly while she is not looking
exercise: Why do bathrooms in the UK and Ireland have pull-cord light switches? | To keep wet hands away from electricity | Because they are cheaper to fit than normal switches | So children can reach them | Because the bulbs are very high
exercise: On holiday in London, an American plug will not fit the socket. What do you do? | Use a proper travel adaptor | Push it in hard until it fits | Bend the pins to fit the holes | Cut the plug off and use the wires
exercise: Which plug has three square pins? | The UK and Irish plug | The American plug, used in New York | The Australian plug, used in Sydney | The Canadian plug, used in Toronto
exercise: You come to a swinging door at school. What do you do? | Open it slowly and look through the glass | Push it hard so it swings open nice and fast | Kick it open with your foot | Run through before it closes
test: A boy in Glasgow puts both hands into the washing-up bowl without looking. Why is that risky? | A sharp knife could be hidden under the bubbles | The water could be too cold | He might drop a cup into the bin | The soap might stain his hands
test: Which is the safe way to clear a blocked food processor? | Turn it off, unplug it, then use a spatula | Reach in quickly between pulses | Push food down with your fingers while it runs slowly | Leave it plugged in but switched off
test: A girl in Phoenix wants a jar from the top shelf and the only thing near is an office chair on wheels. What should she do? | Get a steady step stool or ask someone tall | Stand on the office chair, very carefully and slowly | Climb up the shelves | Use the chair with someone holding the jar
test: Which person is ready to stir a pan on a gas hob? | One with sleeves rolled up and hair tied back | One in a long loose dressing gown with wide sleeves | One with long hair falling forward | One in a scarf hanging down
test: What happens to a full glass bottle of water left in a freezer overnight? | It may crack, because ice takes more space than water | It stays the same, since glass is strong | The water disappears | It shrinks and gets lighter
test: In Anchorage in winter, a child is dared to lick a frozen metal pole. What should he do? | Refuse, as his wet tongue would freeze and stick | Lick it very quickly, so that it has no time to stick | Lick it with his gloves on | Breathe on it first and then lick it
test: A phone charger is plugged in next to a full bath. What is the danger? | If it falls in, the person in the bath can get a deadly electric shock | The bath water will go cold | The phone will charge more slowly | The steam will turn the charger off
test: A family from Toronto travels to Melbourne. What do they need for their chargers? | A travel adaptor for slanted pins | Nothing, since plugs are the same everywhere | A three-square-pin adaptor | A stronger push on the plug
test: Why should you push a swinging door slowly? | Someone may be on the other side | Doors break if they are pushed too slowly | Fast pushing saves energy | Slow doors make no noise
test: can i reach in the washing up bowl, theres a knife in there somewhere | No, a hidden knife can cut you | Yes, feel around carefully | Yes, knives are blunt in water | Yes, if the water is hot
test: smoothie's stuck in the blender, gonna push it with a spoon while its on | Switch it off and unplug it first | Yes, push it gently and it'll be fine | Use your finger instead, it's quicker | Shake the blender hard while it's running
test: biscuits on the top shelf, gonna stand on the swivel chair | Use a steady step stool or ask someone tall | The swivel chair is fine | Climb up the counter edge | Stack two chairs
test: going to Spain, my plug won't fit, just push it in hard | Use a proper travel adaptor | Push it in harder till it clicks | Bend the pins to fit | Cut off the plug
explain: A toddler in Kentucky pulled on a kettle cord that hung over the counter; boiling water spilled on him and he had serious burns that needed weeks in hospital.
explain: A boy in England poked a knife into a toaster to free a stuck slice while it was still plugged in; he got an electric shock that threw him back, and he was lucky not to be killed.
fact: Hot drinks and kettles are among the most common causes of serious burns in small children.
fact: Metal in a plugged-in toaster or socket can give a shock that stops the heart.
test: toast stuck, gonna get it out with a fork | Unplug it first and ask an adult | Yes, quickly before it burns, it's fine | Use a knife instead | Shake it while it's on
test: Why must kettle cords not hang over the edge of the counter? | A small child can pull it and be scalded | The cord gets dusty | The water cools faster | The kettle gets lost
test: lil bro's hand got splashed with hot water | Cool it under cool running water and get an adult | Put butter on it | Put ice right on it | Pop the blister
test: my mum's mate said her kid licked an ice lolly straight from the freezer and his tongue got stuck and ripped off. what's probably blown up? | Ripped off; but wet skin really can stick to very cold things | That ice lollies are cold | That freezers make things freeze | That tongues are wet
test: did u hear?? a boy stuck a fork in the toaster to get his toast out and got such a shock he went to hospital. real lesson? | Never put metal in a plugged-in toaster | Use a knife instead of a fork, it's thinner | Only do it when the toast is done | Wear gloves when you do it
test: apparently a can of coke exploded in someone's freezer and it was like a bomb, the door flew off. what's the true lesson? | Water expands as it freezes, so cans can burst | Fizzy drinks are far too dangerous to keep in a house | Freezers explode if overfilled | Only coke does this, not water
exam: Identify the action required before anything is placed inside a blender. | It is switched off and unplugged | It is set to the slowest speed available | It is filled with water to the brim | It is tilted so as to drain it fully
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises water upon freezing? | It expands, and a sealed can may thereby burst | It contracts and consequently occupies less space | It neither expands nor contracts | It becomes heavier and sinks
exam: Select the response consistent with established safety practice concerning electrical appliances near water. | Mains appliances are not used near a full bath or sink | Hair dryers may be used in the bath provided the hands are dry | Electricity is safe where the water is warm | Plugs may be touched with wet hands
exam: Ascertain the item that is among the most common causes of serious burns in small children. | Hot drinks and kettles | Cold water from the tap | Paper and cardboard in the home | Ice cubes from the freezer
